messages:
Mike: quick Q — running into a TF ordering race when wiring IRSA -> IAM role -> KMS. TL;DR: SA annotation lands before iam role/key policy is applied and pods get decrypt errors. anyone seen a neat pattern to make TF apply reliably?

Mike: current snippets:
```hcl
resource "aws_iam_openid_connect_provider" "oidc" { ... }
resource "aws_iam_role" "pod_role" {
  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.irsa.json
}
resource "kubernetes_service_account" "svc" {
  metadata {
    name = "my-svc"
    namespace = "payments"
    annotations = {
      "eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn" = aws_iam_role.pod_role.arn
    }
  }
}
resource "aws_kms_key" "db_key" { ... }
resource "aws_kms_grant" "sa_grant" {
  key_id = aws_kms_key.db_key.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.pod_role.arn
  operations = ["Decrypt","GenerateDataKey"]
}
```

Mike: problem is sometimes kubernetes_service_account gets applied (with annotation referencing role arn) before the IAM role exists (because of pipeline parallelism), then controller creates short-lived creds but assume fails. I tried `depends_on` but the k8s provider can't reference the role resource easily in another provider step.

Anita: hmm. couple ideas: 1) create the IAM role first (separate apply step) then apply k8s SA. 2) use `data \"aws_iam_role\"` in TF for the role ARN if role is created out-of-band. 3) for in-one-run, add a `null_resource` that forces ordering: make k8s provider resource depend_on that null_resource which depends_on iam role. ugly but works.

Oscar: +1 to separate apply for infra-critical bits. Another option: annotate SA with the role ARN string literal (no interpolation) if you know it ahead — less dynamic but avoids circular.

Sarah: for the KMS side: prefer `aws_kms_grant` over key policy changes when granting to roles used by pods — grants are easier to manage and don't require key policy edits, but note grants can be revoked on rekey/rotation and are ephemeral in some edge cases. If you must change key policy, ensure key policy references the role ARN and tf resource ordering ensures iam role exists before policy attachment.

Mike: thanks — couple follow-ups: 1) any recommended operations set for grant? 2) examples for null_resource hack?

Sarah: ops: ["Decrypt","Encrypt","GenerateDataKey","GenerateDataKeyWithoutPlaintext"] usually covers typical envelope encryption. If you only need decrypt, trim to Decrypt + GenerateDataKey* if using generate.

Oscar: null_resource example (very terse):
```hcl
resource "null_resource" "iam_ready" {
  depends_on = [aws_iam_role.pod_role]
}
resource "kubernetes_service_account" "svc" {
  metadata { ... }
  depends_on = [null_resource.iam_ready]
}
```
Works because null_resource can bridge cross-provider ordering. Not pretty but reliable for one-off migrations.

Anita: FYI also watch the OIDC provider and assume_role_policy condition: be sure `StringEquals` has the correct `sub` value like `system:serviceaccount:payments:my-svc`. If the role's assume policy is too loose it can be exploited; if it's too strict it won't match.

infra-bot: reminder: there is an internal runbook on IRSA ordering in #eng-infra-playbooks — search for \"irsa ordering\" or see https://intra.redwood.dev/infra/irsa-ordering (internal).

Mike: great, I'll try a two-step apply in CI for the iam + kms grant, then do k8s apply. I'll also switch to explicit aws_kms_grant and add the null_resource fallback in the terraform module. thanks all :thumbsup:
